WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.060
Least privilege applies to every queue role.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.320
Producers should only publish the message types and destinations they need.

3
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:12.780
Consumers should only read the queues, topics, or subscriptions required for their workflow.

4
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.960
Administrators should have separate access from application runtimes.

5
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:26.100
Where the platform supports it, separate publish, consume, inspect, replay, purge, delete, configure, and administer permissions.

6
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:30.280
Those operations have very different risk profiles.

7
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.960
Avoid shared high-privilege broker credentials across unrelated services.

8
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:45.540
If every worker uses the same connection string, token, certificate, or service identity, one compromised component can become a path into the entire queue environment.

9
00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:55.400
A worker that sends invoice emails should not automatically be able to publish billing events, delete queues, read administrative messages, or replay high-impact jobs.

10
00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:03.660
The same principle applies to downstream resources: a queue consumer should not inherit broad database, storage, or API permissions it does not need.

11
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.340
Broker credentials are secrets.

12
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:24.920
Connection strings, service account tokens, certificates, passwords, and managed identities should be protected through approved secret-management mechanisms, not copied into source code, committed environment files, container images, deployment packages, logs, tickets, or screenshots.

13
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:34.540
Rotating broker credentials should be possible without rebuilding the whole application from scratch, and teams should know which producers and consumers depend on each credential.

14
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:43.220
Permissions should be revisited when message types, consumers, storage paths, tenants, or downstream actions change.

15
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:48.920
A queue that once handled low-impact work may later carry account changes or file processing requests.

16
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.060
A consumer that once read one queue may gain new responsibilities.

17
00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:06.240
Infrastructure permissions reduce blast radius, but application-level authorization is still required when a message affects users, tenants, documents, payments, or administrative state.
